WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.960
This is the story of an 
evolutionary trap.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.240
A trap laid by people, but which led to the 
demise of thousands of butterflies.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.520
What went wrong?
And can we stop it happening again?

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.480
We start in a meadow in Nevada
with the Edith's Checkerspot butterfly.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.900
Like many butterflies, the Edith's Checkerspot has favourite plants on which to lay its eggs.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.500
in this meadow the butterflies' first
choice is called the Blue-eyed Mary.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.640
The butterflies lay their
eggs in the spring.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.360
A fortnight later they hatch, 
and the ravenous

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.980
caterpillars feed for two weeks, before
the plant starts to die back

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.640
and the caterpillars
enter a dormant state.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.200
Next spring, 
when the Blue-eyed Marys regrow,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.880
the caterpillars wake up, 
gorge themselves,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.880
and build a cocoon.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.740
Then they finally emerge as a butterfly.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.420
This cycle has been going on 
for generations,

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.740
until humans brought in cattle.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:22.980
And with the cows came an
invasive plant called Ribwort Plantain.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.420
The Plantain doesn't die back in the
summer like the Blue-eyed Mary

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.240
so caterpillars which hatch on the
Plantain have longer to eat before

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:36.380
entering their dormant state, helping
more of them survive to adulthood.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.760
What's more, a mother butterfly with a
preference for Plantain

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.320
is likely to pass that preference
on to her caterpillars.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.240
The evolutionary trap was set.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.680
Gradually, the whole population of butterflies
became dependent on the Plantain,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.100
abandoning the 
Blue-eyed Mary entirely.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.580
The butterflies did well, 
for a while...

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.980
Until in 2005, 
the cattle ranchers moved out.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.120
Without the cows, grass quickly grew,
engulfing the Plantain

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.140
and hiding it from
the butterflies.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.800
Without the Plantain to lay their eggs on 
the butterflies were done for.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.960
They couldn't adapt fast enough
to switch back to the Blue-eyed Mary.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.640
Within a year they all died.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:26.640
For four years there were no more
Edith's Checkerspots in the meadow.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.740
But that wasn't the end...

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.200
Luckily, butterflies from other meadows which
never abandoned the Blue-eyed Mary

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.160
moved in, and the population
is now recovering.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.800
This is the first time that this kind of 
evolutionary trap has been recorded,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.440
and around the world, more traps
like it could already be set

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.260
waiting to go off.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.340
Conservationists normally look for
problems when humans arrive,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.580
but this trap was sprung
when people left.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.900
It's important to remember that
humans make changes quickly,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.880
but evolution needs longer to react.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.680
And the researchers hope
that the tale of the Edith's Checkerspot

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.380
will act as a warning

